deadly stunts an obsessed Beetle and a  game-changing mission to Mars This is  the incredible story of 2001 A Space  Odyssey throughout his career Stanley  Kubrick clearly enjoyed adapting novels  into movies he drew on Source material  ranging from pop fiction such as Stephen  King's The Shining to more serious  literary fare like Vladimir nabokov's  Lolita like many of Kubrick's other  works 2001 is based on a book unlike the  other adaptations however the  novelization of 2001 was written  alongside the film's production inspired  to write a movie about space travel and  all its possibilities Kubrick decided to  work with the legendary sci-fi author  Arthur C Clarke the two men agreed to  work together on a story which clock  would turn into a novel while Kubrick  made it into a movie while both Clark  and Kubrick were creative Geniuses in  their own right they had a hard time  actually finishing the film's official  screenplay according to a 1966 New  Yorker profile on the making of 2001  Kubrick estimated that he and Clark  spent an average of four hours a day six  days a week writing the script however  the film's release was delayed until  1968 as some of the plot's finer details  had to be worked out on set whether or  not there was a definitive method to  their Madness Kubrick Clark and Company  wound up with one of Cinema's most  enduring sci-fi stories  The Narrative thrust behind 2001 is the  idea that sooner or later humans are  going to have a close encounter of the  Third Kind it was a timely concept to  explore as during the movie's early  production stages in 1964 a NASA space  probe named the Mariner 4 was on its way  past Mars Kubrick was so confident the  discovery of extraterrestrial life was  imminent he began to fret that NASA  would encounter something on the red  planet before his movie was finished  worried at the possibility of losing  creative license on a story about NASA  Pilots meeting alien intelligence in  deep space Kubrick decided to check if  he could take out an insurance policy on  his own story somehow Kubrick actually  found a company that would offer him  insurance and so he bought the policy  despite its Galactic price tag while the  human race is apparently yet to meet  extraterrestrials it's safe to say that  Kubrick was most definitely a believer  Kubrick found his first hit directing  1960s historical epic Spartacus he  followed that success with a black  comedy about the existential threat born  from the nuclear arms race Dr  Strangelove the kneeless classic is a  hilarious yet devastating satire that's  often imitated but has never been truly  matched I don't know if you can't fight  in here this is the War Room Kubrick had  been inspired to make Dr Strangelove  after researching nuclear defense  policies and taking them to their  logical Extremes in his 1966 New Yorker  profile on Kubrick Jeremy Bernstein  quotes the director is saying the  nuclear strategists who make up all  those War scenarios are never as  inventive as reality and political and  military leaders are never as  sophisticated as they think they are  Bleak stuff for sure but it's entirely  in line with the central thesis of  doctor Strangelove however Bernstein  argued that Kubrick found what he  referred to as limited optimism in the  Space Race Kubrick apparently felt that  the opportunities presented by space  exploration and alien contact just might  save the human species from itself his  belief in the power of space exploration  is all over 2001 from its beautiful  soundtrack to its depiction of man's  divorce from Earthbound technology  Kubrick clearly thought that Mankind's  Ventures into space were full of  potential depicting the future on screen  is no easy feat in order to create a  future audiences may find plausible  Kubrick formed a think tank consisting  of 35 artists and designers a special  effects team more than 20 strong and  several Consulting scientific advisors  this platoon of forward thinkers worked  to ensure the designs of everything felt  futuristic in a believable Way by most  accounts it's fair to say they succeeded  the film's aesthetic is stunning and  some of the choices made by Kubrick and  his design crew are still popular to  this day take for example the movie's  use of RNA Jacobson's flatware a brand  made famous by 2001 that is still bought  and sold today clearly Kubrick's  attention to detail paid dividends while  many film goers may not remember a full  detailed play-by-play of the movie  sprawling plot they'll likely remember  the Innovative aesthetic who were  curated to bring his vision to life one  of the most iconic designs in 2001 is  the Discovery one the NASA exploratory  vessel headed to Jupiter in search of an  alien radio signal in the film Kubrick  handles the spacecraft's introduction  with a level of care usually reserved  for human protagonists its Grand Design  fills the screen as it drifts slowly  into frame then the movie shows off the  ship's distinctive interior a centrifuge  decorated with beds workstations a  dining table computers and more Kubrick  and his advisors felt that long voyages  through space would require artificial  gravity to keep astronauts healthy many  including Kubrick also agreed that  gravity could be easily created in space  with the aid of a centrifuge so the  director hired an engineering company to  build one inside the giant centrifuge  the technology of space flight is seen  at close range the finished product  could spin up to 3 miles an hour cost  three hundred thousand dollars and came  in at about 38 feet in diameter the end  result is not only one of the most  Innovative sets ever designed but a  wonderful symbol for Kubrick's ultimate  Vision like 2001 itself the centrifuge  set is large expensive and very hard to  forget  in one of the film's most thrilling  sequences Dave Bowman finds himself  locked outside of the discovery 1 in a  small maintenance pop Bowman considers  leaving his pod space walking to the  main ship and climbing back inside but  he doesn't have his helmet to get back  into the Ship Without suffocating in  space Bowman fires himself through the  pod's escape hatch into an open airlock  it's an ingenious scene that only runs  about 90 seconds in total in fact the  shot of Bowman launching into the open  airlock is even shorter but that single  shot was extremely dangerous actor care  delay had to dive head first through  Breakaway boards from a platform two  stories above the airlock set falling  down toward an upward tilted camera to  convincingly create the illusion that he  was launching through weightless space  for safety he was secured with a single  rope connected to a pulley an enormous  film camera was positioned at the bottom  of the airlock set to capture the image  in a single take if something had gone  wrong dulay would have crashed directly  into the camera and likely been killed  in the process delay later told Vanity  Fair I think that was the only time on  2001 I got something on the first take  thank God  the dawn of man sequence that kicks off  2001 may be one of the boldest openings  ever depicted in a science fiction movie  in a wordless 10-minute sequence Kubrick  and Company recreate primordial Earth to  present the concept of extraterrestrial  beings jump starting the evolution from  ape to human famously the end of the  sequence season 8 tossing a bone into  the air which slowly transforms into a  satellite orbiting Earth Kubrick  originally tried to film the sequence on  location in Africa but eventually wound  up creating the set in a sound stage in  Britain with a technique known as front  projection the burgeon and camera system  allowed creatives to project  photographic Vistas directly into their  shot however new cameras weren't the  only trick up Kubrick's sleeve the  design team were also tasked with  designing costumes for the Apes despite  their best efforts the sequence went  largely ignored come award season  somewhat ironically the best costume  design Oscar went to Planet of the Apes  damn you thank you  oh God damn you hold the hell while 2001  became the biggest box office earner of  the year when it was released in 1968  MGM took quite a leap of faith when they  originally decided to back Kubrick's  project Kubrick first sent MGM Arthur  Clark's 250-page draft narrative rather  than a finished screenplay and yet  Studio president Robert O'Brien still  agreed to back the filmmaker's vision  given the film's many delays it's safe  to assume O'Brien's confidence may have  been tested along the way mgm's original  press release for the film claimed that  2001 was to be ready for release by  1966. the movie was delayed until 1968  and in 66 the movie's budget increased  from 6 million to 7 million dollars in  the end the film cost MGM around 10  million dollars almost double its  initial budget even though the film  eventually found success over the course  of its release year The Saga concluded  with a shareholder volt according to the  New York Times MGM was also making  another expensive film Dr Zhivago around  the same time with the company facing  such a financially uncertain future  O'Brien wound up resigning as president  in 1969. even though 2001 is widely  regarded as a stone cold classic today  initial reactions from movie critics  were not particularly kind legendary  movie critic Pauline kale said the film  was ponderous and blurry in her review  for Harpers in the New York Times critic  Renata Adler said the movie's  uncompromising slowness rendered the  film hard to sit through without talking  well I don't think there is any question  about it  it can only be attributable to human  error Kubrick was clearly bothered by  the early negative press in a 1968  interview with Playboy the director  expressed his annoyance insisting that  only New York had really been hostile to  the movie he went on to blame the  sensibilities of a cohort he referred to  as New York City's lump in Literati for  their own aversion to his movie since  the expression of grandeur and Cosmic  Wonder while 2001 certainly marches to  its own deliberate Pace it's safe to say  that the movie has aged much better than  its initial reviews today for example it  holds a top 20 spot on the American Film  institute's 100 greatest American films  of all time list  2001 may not have been an immediate hit  with the critics but it very quickly  gained cult status with hippies in fact  the movie was so popular with pillars of  the 60s counterculture community that  the new yorker's 50th Anniversary  retrospective argues that it may have  been a hippie audience that saved the  film from obscurity supposedly fans of  the film consumed psychedelic drugs in  sync with Dave Bowman's trip through the  colorful chaotic Wormhole at the climax  of the film mgm's marketing gurus  eventually got wind of the phenomenon  and added a new tagline to posters the  ultimate trip but word of mouth and  ultimate trips weren't the only thing  bolstering the film's popularity it also  received endorsement from Rock Legends  such as David Bowie and John Lennon  Lennon specifically liked it so much  that he claimed to have seen it every  week during its initial theatrical run  2001 didn't just inspire a generation of  film goers to look up to the stars and  wonder about the future it also inspired  tons of today's best filmmakers for the  movie's 50th Anniversary Indie wire  collected thoughts from a collection of  famous filmmakers on Kubrick's Master  entry to the Sci-Fi Canon the list  featured praise from directors including  Steven Spielberg Claire Dany Christopher  Nolan James Cameron George Lucas Ridley  Scott and Alfonso coron film fans may  not be particularly surprised by this  collection of acolytes as Kubrick's  lasting impact on the industry cannot be  undersold however the impact of 2001  lives on outside of the elite of  Blockbuster filmmakers a 50th  Anniversary retrospective and vulture  credits the film for opening up  possibilities for headier more complex  sci-fi in the Indie realm on top of that  any sci-fi movie about nefarious  artificial intelligence made since 1968  owes a serious debt to the original  murderous computer Hal 9000. open the  pod bay doors hell  I'm sorry Dave I'm afraid I can't do  that clearly Kubrick went on an odyssey  of his own to get 2001 made and perfect  his vision in the end though he  introduced audiences to a whole new  cosmic possibilities at the movies and  Beyond
 

